  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A few showers possible overnight, then likely \nwidespread convection Saturday into Saturday night.\n\nThe slow moving frontal boundary has now pushed into North\nCarolina. A few showers are drifting eastward across central\nVirginia, generally in a weakening state. The bulk of the\novernight hours, and possibly through midday Saturday, may be\ndry in most locations. However, by the second half of tonight,\n850 mb winds become southerly, and overrunning moisture could \nproduce a few light showers or some drizzle. Guidance also \nindicates potential for fog overnight. While some radiation fog \ncould form over northwestern parts of the area where it is still\nmostly clear, it appears the main driver of reduced visibility \nwill be lowering stratus, particularly close to the ridges. Most\nlocations will have overnight lows in the 60s. \n\nSurface high pressure over southern New England slides offshore \nSaturday causing southeasterly flow to develop/strengthen over the \nMid-Atlantic. The synoptic pattern gets messy into the weekend as an \nelongated trough stretches from the Deep South to Mid-Atlantic. The \nstalled boundary over southern VA lifts north as a warm front \nSaturday morning, bringing a possibly first round of showers to the \narea. As the boundary lifts north of the MD/PA border, warm sector \nshowers are possible at time Saturday afternoon, though the bulk of \nconvection is set to arrive toward the evening hours.\n\nNumerous embedded shortwaves lift across the Tennessee Valley toward \nthe southern Mid-Atlantic, helping produce ample lift and shear \nacross the region. A surge of tropical moisture brings PWATs to \naround 2\" Saturday afternoon into Saturday night. A wave of surface \nlow pressure may try to develop somewhere in lee of the Alleghenies \nSaturday evening as the strongest shortwave traverses the region. A \nsubtle jet streak develops ahead of the upper trough, and while not \noverly impressive it will be enough to aid in widespread showers and \nthunderstorm development. Locally heavy rain is likely in some \nareas, though the 12Z high res guidance does not point to any one \nspecific location. Later analysis on areas of low-level convergence \nthat set up will be key to determine where a flash flood threat \ncould develop. But for now, have not issued any Flood Watches due to \nuncertainty in location of heaviest rain. WPC has expanded the \nSlight Risk area for excessive rainfall to include the Alleghenies \nwhere soils are more saturated from rain over the past month. Will \ncontinue to monitor the situation as new mesoscale guidance arrives.\n\nA cold front associated with the upper trough finally swings through \nthe area Sunday morning to afternoon, bringing an end to any \nlingering showers. This front won't bring much of an airmass change \nas temps still reach the 80s and humidity lingers.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions expected early next week ahead \nof a midweek cold front. \n\nUpper-level ridging begins to build over the Southeast beginning \nMonday and into Tuesday as the cold front transitions offshore. \nHumidity & temperatures decrease in the wake of the cold front on \nMonday, with dewpoints ranging in the 50s and low 60s as mid-level \ndry air transitions overhead. Monday night might be the first \nfall night of widespread lows in the 50s.\n\nOverall, anticipate seasonal, dry conditions to persist over the \nfollowing days as this ridging continues to build into the midweek \nperiod. Beginning Wednesday, this ridging begins to transition into \nmore of a zonal flow overhead as a trough further north forms over \nQuebec and Nova Scotia. An associated cold front could begin to \ntrack across the East Coast sometime during this period, although \ncurrent guidance shows some spread in the timing of this frontal \npassage. This front could bring some showers and thunderstorms to \nthe region, but probability of severe weather remains low at this \ntime.",
